# Route Constants Usage

## Core Principle: Never Use Hardcoded Route Paths

All route paths must be defined in `packages/core/constants/routes.ts` and referenced through the exported constants.

## Route Constants Structure

### Available Route Constants

```typescript
import { 
  ROUTES, 
  DASHBOARD_ROUTES, 
  OFFICE_ROUTES, 
  AUTH_ROUTES 
} from '@app/core/constants/routes'

// Dashboard routes
DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath        // "/dashboard"
DASHBOARD_ROUTES.PROFILE.fullPath      // "/dashboard/profile"
DASHBOARD_ROUTES.SETTINGS.fullPath     // "/dashboard/settings"
DASHBOARD_ROUTES.Discover.fullPath     // "/dashboard/discover"

// Office routes (admin only)
OFFICE_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath           // "/office"
OFFICE_ROUTES.JOBS.fullPath            // "/office/jobs"

// Auth routes
AUTH_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath             // "/auth"
AUTH_ROUTES.CONFIRM.fullPath           // "/auth/confirm"
```

## Usage Patterns

### ❌ Bad - Hardcoded Paths

```typescript
// DON'T DO THIS
<Link href="/dashboard">Dashboard</Link>
<Link href="/office/jobs">Manage Jobs</Link>

router.push('/dashboard')
router.replace('/office')

const isActive = pathname === '/dashboard'
```

### ✅ Good - Using Route Constants

```typescript
// DO THIS
import { DASHBOARD_ROUTES, OFFICE_ROUTES } from '@app/core/constants/routes'

<Link href={DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath}>Dashboard</Link>
<Link href={OFFICE_ROUTES.JOBS.fullPath}>Manage Jobs</Link>

router.push(DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath)
router.replace(OFFICE_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath)

const isActive = pathname === DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX.fullPath
```

### Using Route Titles

```typescript
// Access route metadata
const title = DASHBOARD_ROUTES.PROFILE.title // "Profile"
const path = DASHBOARD_ROUTES.PROFILE.fullPath // "/dashboard/profile"
```

## Adding New Routes

When adding new routes, update `packages/core/constants/routes.ts`:

```typescript
// 1. Add to route config
const routeConfig: Record<string, RouteConfig> = {
  // ... existing routes
  
  newSection: {
    path: "/new-section",
    title: "New Section",
    isProtected: true,
    children: {
      index: {
        path: "/new-section",
        title: "New Section Home",
      },
      subsection: {
        path: "/new-section/subsection",
        title: "Subsection",
      },
    },
  },
}

// 2. Export convenience accessor
export const NEW_SECTION_ROUTES = {
  INDEX: ROUTES.newSection.childrenArray?.find((r) => r.path === "/new-section"),
  SUBSECTION: ROUTES.newSection.childrenArray?.find((r) => r.path === "/new-section/subsection"),
}
```

## Special Cases

### Root Path

```typescript
// Root path is always "/"
if (href === "/") {
  return pathname === "/" || pathname === "/index"
}
```

### Path Matching in Utils

Use route constants for path matching:

```typescript
import { DASHBOARD_ROUTES, OFFICE_ROUTES } from '@app/core/constants/routes'

// Exact path matching
const dashboardPath = DASHBOARD_ROUTES.INDEX?.fullPath || "/dashboard"
if (href === dashboardPath) {
  return pathname === dashboardPath || pathname === `${dashboardPath}/index`
}
```

## Benefits

1. **Type Safety** - TypeScript will catch typos and missing routes
2. **Single Source of Truth** - All paths defined in one place
3. **Easier Refactoring** - Change path in one place, updates everywhere
4. **Better IDE Support** - Autocomplete and navigation
5. **Documentation** - Route metadata (title, description) in one place

## Enforcement

- **ALWAYS import route constants** when working with navigation
- **NEVER use string literals** for route paths (except in routes.ts itself)
- **ALWAYS use `.fullPath`** property to get the actual path string
- **DOCUMENT new routes** with proper titles and descriptions

## Migration

When finding hardcoded paths:

1. Identify the path and its purpose
2. Check if it exists in `routes.ts`
3. If not, add it to `routes.ts` with proper structure
4. Replace hardcoded string with route constant reference
5. Verify the change works correctly
